  <body data-trk-page="atc">
    <main class="container">
      <div class="trk-nav">
        <a href="/">&larr; The Blue Board</a> / <a href="/trackers">Trackers</a> / Modern Skies
      </div>

      <header class="trk-header trk-section">
        <div class="trk-kicker">Tracker &middot; FAA Modernization</div>
        <h1>Is Your Airport Off Paper Yet?</h1>
        <p class="trk-subtitle">
          Most US airport towers still track your flight on strips of paper, passed by hand. The FAA
          is finally replacing them at 89 airports — this page tracks every one: who's gone digital,
          who's scheduled, and who's still waiting for a date.
        </p>
        <div class="trk-updated-row">
          <LastUpdated iso={atcMeta.lastUpdated} />
        </div>
        <nav class="trk-jump" aria-label="Section navigation">
          <a href="#map">The map</a>
          <a href="#check">Check your airport</a>
          <a href="#all">All 89 airports</a>
          <a href="#whats-changing">What's changing</a>
          <a href="#paper-strips">Why paper?</a>
          <a href="#changelog">Changelog</a>
        </nav>
      </header>

      <section class="trk-section" aria-label="Headline numbers">
        <StatStrip stats={stats} />
      </section>

      <section class="trk-section" id="map">
        <h2>The map: paper vs. pixels</h2>
        <TrackerMap
          paths={paths}
          points={mapPoints}
          legend={legend}
          offMap={offMap}
          ariaLabel={`US map of the FAA electronic flight strips rollout: ${liveCount} airports live, ${plannedCount} scheduled, ${paperNoDateCount} with no date. Full details in the table below.`}
          hint="Hover or tap a dot for details — full list in the table below"
        />
      </section>

      <section class="trk-section" id="check">
        <h2>Check your airport</h2>
        <TrackerSearch
          label="Search the program"
          noun="airports"
          placeholder="Airport code or city — try AUS, O'Hare, Boise&hellip;"
          emptyTitle="Not in the program"
          emptyBody="No match in the FAA's 89-airport electronic-strips program. Smaller towers keep their current systems for now — the program covers the country's busiest airports first, and the FAA hasn't published a plan beyond these 89."
        />
      </section>

      <section class="trk-section" id="all">
        <h2>All 89 airports</h2>
        <TrackerTable
          caption="FAA TFDM rollout status for all 89 program airports"
          columns={columns}
          rows={tableRows}
        />
        <p class="trk-method">
          Dates marked <em>2023 plan</em> are the airport's slot in the FAA's last published
          schedule (August 2023, via the DOT Inspector General) — sequencing, not commitments.
          Washington National jumped its slot by two and a half years; Boston's has come and gone.
          Where a live airport shows no date, the FAA never published one: it announces go-lives
          selectively, so this column is built from its audit trail and press coverage.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="trk-section" id="whats-changing">
        <h2>What's actually changing</h2>
        <p>
          In July 2025, Congress handed the FAA
          <a href="https://www.aopa.org/news-and-media/all-news/2025/july/10/where-is-the-12-5-billion-for-atc-modernization-going">$12.5 billion</a>
          to modernize air traffic control — new fiber to replace 1970s copper phone lines, new
          radios, new radar, and the end of paper flight strips. Everyone involved calls it a down
          payment: the industry's Modern Skies Coalition — 66 aviation groups —
          <a href="https://ttd.org/policy/letters-to-congress/modern-skies-coalition-urges-congress-to-close-funding-gap-for-brand-new-air-traffic-control-system-bnatcs/">asked Congress for another $20 billion</a>
          in July 2026. That part hasn't been appropriated.
        </p>
        <p>
          The build is split among a handful of companies.
          <a href="https://www.peraton.com/news/faa-awards-peraton-a-modernization-contract-to-build-the-brand-new-air-traffic-control-system">Peraton</a>
          is the integrator running the overall program — its own contract is
          <a href="https://www.highergov.com/contract/693KA726C00001/">capped at $1.5 billion</a>,
          not the "$12.5 billion" some headlines claimed. L3Harris is rebuilding the network and
          <a href="https://www.l3harris.com/newsroom/editorial/2026/05/l3harris-reaches-over-50-faa-telecommunications-modernization">passed the halfway mark</a>
          in May 2026. RTX and Indra are replacing
          <a href="https://www.flyingmag.com/contractors-replace-1980s-atc-radar/">up to 612 radars</a>
          by June 2028. And Leidos builds the piece this page tracks: TFDM, the system that
          replaces paper strips.
        </p>
        <h3>What a tower actually gets</h3>
        <p>
          Today, a controller works your flight on a printed strip, marks it in pencil, and walks it
          to the next position. TFDM replaces that with electronic strips on touchscreens — shared
          instantly between controllers, traffic managers, and airline ops. At the biggest airports
          it adds surface metering: instead of everyone pushing back and idling in a taxi queue, the
          system holds you at the gate and releases you into a moving line. Less burned fuel, more
          honest departure times.
        </p>
        <h3>The pace, honestly</h3>
        <p>
          Eighteen towers went digital in the first 44 months — about five per year. The current
          promise is a different order of magnitude:
          <a href="https://fedscoop.com/faa-digital-flight-strip-system-atc-modernization/">Leidos says another dozen airports by the end of 2026</a>,
          and the FAA says all 89 by 2028 — roughly a six-fold acceleration that started showing up
          in 2026, with Washington National jumping a 2028 slot to go live in February. Worth
          holding next to that: the DOT Inspector General's
          <a href="https://www.oig.dot.gov/library-item/46321">2024 audit</a> found the program's
          first decade cut its scope from 89 towers to 49 while costs rose, and estimated
          completion in <em>2030</em> — for the smaller plan. The 2025 money put all 89 back on the
          board. This tracker exists to show which trajectory is real, one airport at a time.
        </p>
        <h3>The skeptics' case</h3>
        <p>
          Congress has seen this movie: NextGen ran two decades,
          <a href="https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-24-105254">$14 billion through 2022</a>, and
          delivered about 16% of its promised benefits by the government's own accounting. At the
          December 2025 oversight hearings, Sen. Tammy Duckworth called the three-year timeline
          "setting the integrator up to fail," and Rep. Troy Nehls asked what guardrails would keep
          this from becoming "another NextGen boondoggle." Even the sunny numbers deserve
          attribution: the claim that DCA deployed "45% faster than the typical cycle" traces to a
          single Leidos press release, and the FAA's own transparency site generates its
          per-airport answers with AI and was still calling Austin "planned" two weeks after the
          ribbon was cut. We use it as a pointer, and verify against the audit trail and press.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="trk-aside" id="paper-strips" aria-label="Why paper strips existed">
        <div class="trk-aside-kicker">Sidebar</div>
        <h2>Why paper strips existed — and why they almost outlived you</h2>
        <p>
          A flight progress strip is a piece of paper about the size of a ruler: one flight, printed
          minutes before it needs attention, slotted into a plastic holder in a rack called a bay.
          As your flight taxis, the controller marks it in a pencil shorthand older than the FAA
          itself — runway assignments, altitudes, handoffs — and when responsibility for your
          aircraft moves to the next position, the strip physically moves with it. That little slide
          of plastic across a console <em>is</em> the handoff.
        </p>
        <p>
          The reason paper survived the jet age, GPS, and the iPhone is that it's genuinely good.
          A strip never crashes, never lags, and never needs a login. It's readable in a glance from
          three feet away in a sunlit tower cab. And the act of writing is the point: controllers
          will tell you that marking a strip is how the picture of the airspace gets into your head.
          When the FAA first tried to kill paper strips, the working name for the replacement dated
          to the <em>1980s</em> — this is at least the third attempt.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the record, the first US tower to actually drop paper was not Phoenix, whatever the
          internet tells you — it was Cleveland, on October 24, 2022, two years behind schedule.
          And somewhere in St. Louis there's a printer currently scheduled to produce America's
          last paper flight strip: STL holds the final slot in the FAA's published sequence, 2029
          in the plan, 2028 in the promise. This page will tell you which one wins.
        </p>
      </section>

